  5.2  HOW TO REQUEST. Write to shuvam@sycolex.com with the subject line
       "Commercial licence request — lammps-web-gui", describing who You
       are, what You intend to build or deliver, how the Software is used,
       the expected scale, and Your pricing model. See COMMERCIAL.md for
       the full checklist.

  5.3  THE AUTHOR'S DISCRETION. The Author may grant, refuse or condition a
       Commercial Licence for any reason, and may attach different terms to
       different licensees.

  5.4  REVENUE SHARE. Unless the signed Commercial Licence states otherwise,
       a granted Commercial Licence carries a royalty of TEN PERCENT (10%)
       of Revenue, payable to the Author. Where the Software is one
       component of a larger offering, the parties will agree in writing on
       an apportionment before the licence takes effect; absent such a
       written apportionment, the royalty applies to the full Revenue of
       the offering. The headline rate is a starting point and may be
       negotiated up or down — academic spin-offs, non-profits and small
       independent developers are explicitly invited to ask for reduced or
       waived terms.

  5.5  REPORTING AND PAYMENT. A commercial licensee shall, within thirty
       (30) days of the end of each calendar quarter, deliver a written
       statement of Revenue for that quarter and pay the royalty due.
       Amounts unpaid after that date accrue interest at 1.5% per month or
       the maximum rate permitted by law, whichever is lower.

  5.6  AUDIT. Once per calendar year, on thirty (30) days' notice, the
       Author or an independent auditor bound to confidentiality may
       inspect the licensee's relevant records. If an audit discovers an
       underpayment exceeding five percent (5%) for the audited period, the
       licensee bears the cost of the audit in addition to the shortfall
       and interest.

  5.7  UNLICENSED COMMERCIAL USE. Commercial Use without a Commercial
       Licence is copyright infringement, terminates this Licence
       automatically under Section 7, and makes You liable for the royalty
       that would have been due on all Revenue earned during the
       infringement, in addition to every other remedy available at law or
       in equity.

